const README = `# Store Notes — setup and deployment\n\nStore Notes is a mobile-first React PWA that talks directly to Supabase. No server or service-role key is used in the browser.\n\n## 1. Supabase\n1. Create a free project at supabase.com and wait for it to finish.\n2. Open SQL Editor, download /setup.sql from the running app (or run npm run setup:sql), paste it, and click Run. This creates tables, indexes, triggers, strict RLS, private photo storage, categories, and sample stores.\n3. In Authentication > Providers, keep Email enabled. In Authentication > Users, create your first user.\n4. Copy that user's UUID. In SQL Editor run the final commented profiles insert from setup.sql, replacing UUID/name/email. Use regional_manager for the first account.\n5. In Project Settings > API, copy Project URL and the publishable/anon key. Never use the service_role key.\n\n## 2. Local configuration\nSet VITE_SUPABASE_URL and VITE_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY in your shell or hosting dashboard. Then run npm install and npm run dev. Without these values the app shows a configuration-required screen and does not load sample users.\n\n## 3. Users and store access\nCreate Auth users in the Supabase dashboard or a separately trusted admin process. Insert a matching profiles row. Assign Area and Store Managers by inserting user_store_access rows. Regional Managers automatically access every store. Browser-side account creation is intentionally omitted because it would require privileged credentials.\n\n## 4. Add the remaining 21 stores\nInsert rows into public.stores with a unique store_code, store_name, area, and district. The sample INSERT near the end of setup.sql is an easy template.\n\n## 5. Production build\nRun npm run build. The static site is written to dist/. Deploy that folder to Vercel, Netlify, GitHub Pages, or another static host. Configure both VITE_ variables before the build. No Node server is needed in production.\n\nNetlify: build command npm run build; publish directory dist. Vercel: framework preset Other; build command npm run build; output directory dist.\n\n## 6. Android installation\nOpen the deployed HTTPS URL in Chrome, sign in, open the browser menu, and choose Install app. The service worker caches the app shell; typed finding drafts are preserved locally when offline. Submissions require connectivity and can be retried.\n\n## Security notes\nRLS is the authorization boundary, not frontend filtering. Store Manager status transitions and protected fields are guarded by a database trigger. The photo bucket is private and limited to authenticated store access. Activity history has no user insert/update/delete policy and is written only by security-definer triggers.\n`;

